---

<DocsLayout title="How it works" pathname="/docs/how-it-works" section="Getting started">
  <h1>How it works</h1>
  <p class="dx-lede">
    Everything Máddu does reduces to one substrate loop. Every surface above it &mdash; slash
    commands, autopilot, pipelines &mdash; is a friendly wrapper over that same loop, never a
    competing flow.
  </p>

  <h2>The substrate loop</h2>
  <p>
    Six transitions bound every unit of work. Each one writes exactly one event to the
    <strong>spine</strong> &mdash; the append-only event log on disk &mdash; so any run
    replays deterministically from the spine alone.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>register</strong> &mdash; a session binds an agent identity to this turn.</li>
    <li><strong>claim</strong> &mdash; a lane locks one file area so two agents never touch the same files.</li>
    <li><strong>slice</strong> &mdash; one unit of work runs under that lane, observable end to end.</li>
    <li><strong>stop</strong> &mdash; the slice closes with a structured receipt and hindsight extraction.</li>
    <li><strong>release</strong> &mdash; the lane frees for the next agent.</li>
    <li><strong>close</strong> &mdash; the session leaves the record cleanly.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On Claude Code this loop is <strong>enforced</strong>, not just asked for: with the
    session hooks installed, a stale ritual &mdash; no session, no lane, an overdue
    <code>slice-stop</code> &mdash; blocks the next mutating edit until you clear it (see
    <a href="/docs/governance">Governance</a>).
  </p>

  <Mermaid
    caption="Spine view — every transition appends, hindsight feeds memory"
    aside="6 transitions · 1 spine"
    code={`
flowchart LR
    A([session register]):::session --> B([claim lane]):::lane
    B --> C([slice of work]):::framework
    C --> D{slice-stop}:::approval
    D -->|release| E([lane release]):::lane
    E --> F([session close]):::session
    D -.hindsight.-> G[[skills + memory]]:::accent
    A -.events.-> S[("append-only<br/>spine")]:::spine
    B -.events.-> S
    C -.events.-> S
    D -.events.-> S
    E -.events.-> S
`}
  />

  <p>Each transition maps to one spine event:</p>

  <div class="dx-code">
    <div class="dx-code-head"><span class="dx-code-dot"></span> spine event</div>
    <pre><code>SESSION_REGISTERED
LANE_CLAIMED
SLICE_STARTED
SLICE_STOPPED
LANE_RELEASED
SESSION_CLOSED</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="dx-note">
    <span class="dx-note-glyph" aria-hidden="true">&#9670;</span>
    <p><strong>Replay is deterministic.</strong> Because state lives only in the append-only spine, past state reconstructs from the log alone &mdash; no live process to trust.</p>
  </div>

  <EventStream
    eyebrow="What lands on disk · sample slice trace"
    title="spine · turn 12"
  />

  <h2>Wrappers over the same loop</h2>
  <p>
    Slash commands and autopilot don&rsquo;t add a second execution model &mdash; they drive
    the substrate for you. <code>/maddu-autopilot ship X</code>, or plain natural language
    like &ldquo;fix the bug in Y,&rdquo; goes in; the agent classifies intent, dispatches the
    right flow, and tells you which one it picked. The verbose CLI stays available underneath
    for scripts and power users.
  </p>

  <h2>Pipelines</h2>
  <p>
    For non-trivial work the agent reaches for a <strong>pipeline</strong> first. A pipeline
    walks the substrate for you &mdash; each stage is a literal <code>maddu</code> verb, and
    pipeline state is itself an event, so re-runs against the same goal are deterministic.
    Three ship by default:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>ship-a-feature</strong> (default) &mdash; end-to-end feature work: orient, plan, coordinate, slice, test, review, land, account.</li>
    <li><strong>fix-a-bug</strong> &mdash; recovery: reproduce, isolate, fix, verify, land.</li>
    <li><strong>plan-and-delegate</strong> &mdash; fan out across disjoint lanes via the coordinator.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ad-hoc autopilot is reserved for genuine one-offs; anything substantial defaults to a pipeline.</p>

  <h2>The coordinator primitive</h2>
  <p>
    Fan-out is a <strong>Máddu primitive</strong>, not a Claude Code or Codex feature.
    <code>maddu coordinator &lt;plan-id&gt;</code> reads a plan, walks its phases, spawns one
    worker per phase, monitors heartbeats, and halts and reports at iteration caps. Every
    transition stamps the spine, so pause-resume is free &mdash; the spine carries the state,
    not the coordinator process.
  </p>
  <p>
    That&rsquo;s what makes Máddu&rsquo;s autonomy <em>portable</em> across runtimes: the loop
    lives on disk, so it doesn&rsquo;t depend on any one agent CLI.
  </p>

  <Mermaid
    caption="Coordinator sequence — the operator never owns the loop, the spine does"
    aside="runtime-agnostic"
    code={`
sequenceDiagram
    participant Op as Operator
    participant Co as Coordinator
    participant Sp as Spine
    participant W1 as Worker · Phase 1
    participant W2 as Worker · Phase 2
    Op->>Co: maddu coordinator <plan>
    Co->>Sp: COORDINATOR_STARTED
    rect rgba(208, 255, 0, 0.06)
    Note over W1,Sp: Phase 1 · lane A
    Co->>W1: spawn(runtime, phase 1)
    W1->>Sp: SESSION_REGISTERED · LANE_CLAIMED · SLICE_STARTED
    W1-->>Co: heartbeat
    W1->>Sp: SLICE_STOPPED + LANE_RELEASED
    Co->>Sp: COORDINATOR_PHASE_COMPLETED
    end
    rect rgba(86, 184, 255, 0.06)
    Note over W2,Sp: Phase 2 · lane B
    Co->>W2: spawn(runtime, phase 2)
    W2->>Sp: SESSION_REGISTERED · LANE_CLAIMED · SLICE_STARTED
    W2->>Sp: SLICE_STOPPED + LANE_RELEASED
    Co->>Sp: COORDINATOR_PHASE_COMPLETED
    end
    Co->>Sp: COORDINATOR_COMPLETED
    Co-->>Op: halt-and-report
`}
  />

